  "final_answer": "I have all the information I need from WolframAlpha. Let me provide the final answer.\n\n## WolframAlpha Solution\n\n**ODE solved:** g'(t) + cos(g(t)) = 0, with g(0) = 1\n\n**Classification:** First-order nonlinear ODE (separable)\n\n**Separable form:** -(g'(t))/cos(g(t)) = 1\n\n### Solution\n\n$$g(t) = -\\sin^{-1}\\!\\left(\\coth\\!\\left(t - \\coth^{-1}(\\sin(1))\\right)\\right)$$\n\nIn Wolfram Language notation: `g[t] == -ArcSin[Coth[t - ArcCoth[Sin[1]]]]`\n\n### Constant Determination\n\nThe constant of integration is $C = \\coth^{-1}(\\sin(1))$, which is determined by the condition $g(0) = 1$:\n\n$$1 = g(0) = -\\arcsin\\!\\left(\\coth\\!\\left(-C\\right)\\right) = -\\arcsin\\!\\left(-\\coth(C)\\right) = \\arcsin\\!\\left(\\coth(C)\\right)$$\n\nTherefore: $\\coth(C) = \\sin(1)$, so $C = \\coth^{-1}(\\sin(1))$.\n\n### Value of the Constant\n\n$$C = \\coth^{-1}(\\sin(1)) = \\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{1+\\sin(1)}{\\sin(1)-1}\\right) = \\ln(\\sec(1) + \\tan(1)) + \\frac{i\\pi}{2} \\approx 1.22627 + 1.57080\\,i$$\n\n(Note: Since $|\\sin(1)| \\approx 0.8415 < 1$, this constant is complex, but the resulting $g(t)$ is real, and $g(0) = \\arcsin(\\sin(1)) = 1$ since 1 radian lies in $[-\\pi/2, \\pi/2]$.)\n\n### Equivalent Real Form\n\nThe same solution can be expressed in a real form as:\n\n$$g(t) = 2\\arctan\\!\\left((\\sec(1)+\\tan(1))\\,e^{-t}\\right) - \\frac{\\pi}{2}$$\n\nwhich clearly satisfies $g(0) = 2\\arctan(\\sec(1)+\\tan(1)) - \\pi/2 = 2(\\pi/4 + 1/2) - \\pi/2 = 1$.",
